  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nOne upper level disturbance/shortwave will track south/east of \nthe region this afternoon, with another poised to track across \nnorthern portions of the region Tuesday afternoon/evening. \nCurrent showers will decrease in coverage this afternoon for \nareas south of I-90, though isolated/scattered showers/sprinkles\nwill persist for areas near and especially north of I-90 \nthrough this evening given cyclonic low level flow, cold air \naloft and lake moisture. West to northwest winds will remain \nbrisk, occasionally gusting 25-35 mph especially within the \nMohawk Valley, Capital Region and Berkshires southward.\n\nBrief low level ridging may allow for some breaks in the clouds\ntonight and Wednesday morning, especially south of I-90 before\nanother shortwave tracks across northern areas Tuesday\nafternoon/evening. Isolated/scattered rain showers will develop\nTuesday afternoon/evening for areas mainly north of I-90 as this\ndisturbance passes through, with some lingering showers in the\nwake of the disturbance due to some lake effect. Winds will\nbecome gusty Tuesday afternoon, especially north of I-90 where\nsouthwest to west winds may reach 25-35 mph, then closer to I-90\nextending into the Capital Region and Berkshires Tuesday evening\nas the shortwave passes. Some gusts could again reach 30-40 mph,\nespecially for some higher terrain areas of Schoharie County,\nthe Helderbergs and Berkshires. \n\nAlthough clouds could linger Wednesday morning, transitory\nridging may allow for some breaks in the clouds across the\nregion Wednesday afternoon along with lighter winds.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nLow confidence forecast for Thursday through early Saturday\nregarding precipitation types, amounts and northern extent as a\nstorm system approaching from the Ohio Valley/northern\nAppalachians Thursday redevelops near or off the northern mid\nAtlantic coast Friday. Depending on how far north the\nredeveloping low tracks, and also how quickly it tracks east\nwill ultimately determine eventual precipitation type and\namounts across the region. Current ensemble spread remains high\non the surface low track/evolution. The most likely outcome at\nthis time would be for periods of rain to develop Thursday, \npossibly mixing with/changing to wet snow across higher terrain \nareas Thursday night into Friday night/Saturday morning. \nHowever, should the re- developing storm track a bit farther \nsouth and quicker, there could be less precipitation and dynamic\ncooling across the region, resulting in less high-elevation \nsnow and also a more limited northern extent of rainfall. Trends\nwill need to be watched as we get closer to the late week \nperiod.\n\nDespite low confidence in precipitation types/amounts,\nconfidence remains high in below normal temperatures for\nThursday through Saturday, with daytime highs mainly in the 30s\nand 40s, and overnight lows in the 30s for most lower elevations\nand 20s to lower 30s across higher terrain.",
